How the Future License conversion works
---------------------------------------

Each release of the platform core is published under FSL-1.1-MIT. On the
second anniversary of the date a given release is made available, that
release converts to the MIT License. The MIT text used by the conversion is
reproduced verbatim in LICENSE-MIT for transparency.

In practice this means:

  - Active development always lives under FSL-1.1-MIT.
  - Each release has a fixed expiry: two years to MIT.
  - The most recent release is always at most two years away from being
    plain MIT — even if upstream development stops, the codebase eventually
    becomes fully OSI-open.

Plugins
-------

First-party and community plugins under providers/, backstage-plugins/, and
the InfrastructureProviderPlugin / ServiceProviderPlugin SDKs are licensed
under the MIT License from day one (no FSL window). Each such directory
carries its own LICENSE file.

Why FSL-1.1-MIT?
----------------

The FSL Permitted-Purpose clause restricts other parties from offering the
platform as a competing managed service while it is under active
development. After two years, that protection naturally relaxes as the
release converts to MIT. This approach follows the model adopted by Sentry
in 2023 and provides a single, well-understood license to communicate.

